Q: gRPC vs REST — when to use which?

────────────────────────────────────────
⚙️ What is gRPC?

gRPC (Google Remote Procedure Call) = high-performance RPC framework.
Services call each other like methods, over **HTTP/2**, with **Protocol Buffers (protobuf)** binary payloads.

Often used for **internal** microservice-to-microservice calls when speed/streaming matter.
Public/browser APIs usually stay REST/JSON.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Compare

| Feature         | gRPC                            | REST (typical)                    |
| --------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Style           | RPC (call methods)              | Resource URLs + HTTP verbs        |
| Payload         | Protobuf (binary, compact)      | JSON (text, human-readable)       |
| Transport       | HTTP/2 (required model)         | HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2                |
| Speed / size    | Usually smaller & faster        | Larger JSON, more parsing         |
| Streaming       | Client / server / bidirectional | Limited (SSE/WebSocket separately)|
| Contract        | `.proto` + codegen              | OpenAPI/Swagger (often manual)    |
| Browser         | Not native (need grpc-web/proxy)| Native fetch/XHR                  |
| Debugging       | Harder (binary)                 | Easy in browser/Postman           |

Important nuance:
REST can also run on HTTP/2.
gRPC’s usual win is **protobuf + native streaming + codegen**, not “REST cannot use HTTP/2.”

────────────────────────────────────────
Why protobuf payloads are smaller

JSON sends field names every time (text):
{ "id": 101, "name": "Ali", "age": 25 }  → larger

Protobuf schema assigns numbers:
message User {
  int32 id = 1;
  string name = 2;
  int32 age = 3;
}
On wire: compact binary tags + values (names not repeated as text).
→ less bandwidth, faster serialize/deserialize.

Bandwidth = how much data the network can carry per second (like highway width).
Smaller messages → less congestion / lower transfer time for same call volume.

## payload size is lesser because unlike json it does not contain key value means each key is duplicate in every number of array in json that is larger in size)
## when its size increase it take more bandwidth

Mbps (Megabits per second)
Gbps (Gigabits per second)
Think of bandwidth as the width of a highway:
A 2-lane road = low bandwidth (fewer cars can pass at once)
An 8-lane highway = high bandwidth (more cars can pass at once)
The cars are your data packets.

────────────────────────────────────────
Sample .proto

syntax = "proto3";

service UserService {
  rpc GetUser(UserRequest) returns (UserResponse);
}

message UserRequest {
  string userId = 1;
}

message UserResponse {
  string name = 1;
  string email = 2;
}

Both sides share this contract → generate Java/Go/Python stubs → type-safe clients/servers.

────────────────────────────────────────
When to use

Use **gRPC** when:
- Internal microservice calls
- High QPS / low latency needed
- Streaming (chat-like, continuous data)
- Polyglot services (Java ↔ Go ↔ Python) with shared `.proto`

Use **REST** when:
- Public APIs
- Browser / mobile simple JSON clients
- Third-party integrations
- Human-debuggable contracts matter more than max performance

Common real architecture:
External clients → REST/HTTPS API Gateway  
Internal services → gRPC (or REST if team prefers simplicity)

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer

“REST is resource-based HTTP + usually JSON — great for public and browser APIs. gRPC is RPC over HTTP/2 with protobuf — smaller binary payloads, codegen,
and first-class streaming, so it’s strong for internal microservice communication.
I’d expose REST outside and consider gRPC inside when performance or streaming matters.”

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

REST = public-friendly JSON APIs; gRPC = fast internal binary RPC + streaming.
